<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dead Sea Scrolls</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Qumran Caves Scrolls, Judaean Desert scrolls</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> Scrolls are a set of ancient Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period, discovered between 1946 and 1956, mostly in the Qumran Caves near the Dead Sea in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. Dating from the 3rd century BCE to the 1st century CE, they include the oldest surviving manuscripts of entire books later included in the Jewish and Christian biblical canons, texts never canonized, and sectarian writings such as the Community Rule and the War Scroll. Most are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and a few in Greek; researchers have assembled 981 different manuscripts from 11 caves. Almost all are held in the Shrine of the Book at the Israel Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls.jpg" alt="Dead Sea Scrolls" width="330" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Israel Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Scroll.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>, found in caves near Qumran from 1947, include copies of books of the Hebrew Bible about a thousand years older than the oldest complete Hebrew manuscripts known before them.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hebrew Bible contains Aramaic chapters in the books of Daniel and Ezra, and the <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b> include Aramaic works, such as fragments of the Book of Enoch.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b>, from the 3rd century BCE to the 1st century CE, are mostly in Hebrew, and letters of Simon bar Kokhba, the leader of the revolt against Rome in 132 to 135 CE, were written in Hebrew as well as in Aramaic and Greek.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ashkar-Gilson Manuscript, a fragment of a 7th-century Torah scroll, already shows the &quot;brickwork&quot; pattern, which some scholars take as evidence that the Masoretes copied earlier manuscripts faithfully; the <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b> do not use it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a></li></ul></section>
